WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.064
Data pipelines read their rows from data sources.

00:00:04.064 --> 00:00:07.908
Your own module can add one, so that its records appear next to

00:00:07.908 --> 00:00:10.582
the content items and the users of a site.

00:00:10.582 --> 00:00:13.980
This video builds a data source for a help desk module.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:23.178
A data source connects one place where data lives, such as the content

00:00:23.178 --> 00:00:27.051
items of a site, its users, or the records of your module.

00:00:27.051 --> 00:00:30.560
It has a technical name, a label, and a description.

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:33.919
A data source exposes data sets.

00:00:33.919 --> 00:00:39.205
A data set is one table, such as the content items of one content type.

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:42.637
Its fields have a name, a label, and a type:

00:00:42.637 --> 00:00:46.550
Text, Integer, Decimal, Boolean, Date, or DateTime.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:51.033
Rows are read one batch at a time, 500 by default, so a

00:00:51.033 --> 00:00:55.807
consumer can read any number of rows without holding them all in memory.

00:00:55.807 --> 00:01:00.520
And data is read for a user, who sees only the data sets they may read.

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:08.764
The Data Pipelines designer is one consumer of data sources.

00:01:08.764 --> 00:01:12.886
Its Add a data source button opens a dialog that lists the data

00:01:12.886 --> 00:01:16.770
sets of every registered data source, for the current user.

00:01:17.253 --> 00:01:21.032
Let's choose the Users data source in the list on the left, to

00:01:21.032 --> 00:01:22.421
see only its cards.

00:01:22.421 --> 00:01:26.366
Each card is a data set, with its label, its description, and the

00:01:26.366 --> 00:01:28.033
name of its data source.

00:01:28.486 --> 00:01:33.371
The Users data source is registered the same way you will register yours.

00:01:33.371 --> 00:01:37.205
It lists its data sets only to users who may view users.

00:01:37.205 --> 00:01:40.606
Add puts a Read a data source step on the canvas.

00:01:41.059 --> 00:01:43.780
The Fields tab shows what the step produces.

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:48.839
Each field has its label from the schema, its technical name in brackets, and its type.

00:01:49.297 --> 00:01:53.612
On the Data tab, Preview reads the first rows through the data source,

00:01:53.612 --> 00:01:55.429
for the user who previews.

00:01:55.429 --> 00:01:59.517
Each column header is a field label, with the field type under it.

00:02:03.717 --> 00:02:08.599
A module that adds a data source depends on the Data Pipelines Core feature.

00:02:08.599 --> 00:02:14.017
That feature is enabled only as a dependency, so enabling your module enables it too.

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:20.336
The simplest data source derives from RecordDataSource and declares IDataSource.

00:02:20.336 --> 00:02:25.840
The static Name, DisplayName, and Description describe it, and each data set is

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.337
a RecordDataSet over a .NET type, here a ticket.

00:02:30.087 --> 00:02:33.653
In the data set, AddField declares each field in order:

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:38.097
its technical name, its label, its type, and a function that reads the

00:02:38.097 --> 00:02:39.618
value from a record.

00:02:39.618 --> 00:02:43.477
Saved pipelines store these names, so they must stay stable.

00:02:44.227 --> 00:02:47.849
IsIdentifier marks a field that identifies a record.

00:02:47.849 --> 00:02:53.658
IsKeyFilterable promises that the data set can read only the records of a list of keys.

00:02:53.658 --> 00:02:59.467
And References names the data set the values identify, so consumers can suggest a join.

00:03:00.217 --> 00:03:05.208
CanReadAsync tells whether the user of the context may read the data set.

00:03:05.208 --> 00:03:10.199
The data source lists, describes, and reads only the data sets that pass.

00:03:10.199 --> 00:03:14.117
A published pipeline reads as the user who published it.

00:03:14.867 --> 00:03:19.200
LoadAsync streams the records in a stable order, one page at a time,

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.719
so any number of records can be read.

00:03:21.719 --> 00:03:26.697
The base class reads only the fields and the number of rows the query asks for.

00:03:27.447 --> 00:03:32.158
DataConditions reads the conditions a store can apply while it loads, such as

00:03:32.158 --> 00:03:34.486
a list of keys or a range of dates.

00:03:34.486 --> 00:03:39.027
The consumer filters again, so a condition can be ignored, except the keys

00:03:39.027 --> 00:03:40.957
of an IsKeyFilterable field.

00:03:45.157 --> 00:03:51.446
Register the data source with AddDataSource, in a startup that requires the Data Pipelines Core feature.

00:03:51.446 --> 00:03:56.077
A data source is a scoped service, and two data sources can't share a name.

00:03:56.827 --> 00:04:00.666
Once your module is enabled, its data sets appear to the users who

00:04:00.666 --> 00:04:04.452
may read them, in the dialog you saw and in every other consumer.

00:04:04.452 --> 00:04:08.237
If the feature that provides it is disabled, a step that reads it

00:04:08.237 --> 00:04:10.317
reports that it is not available.

00:04:14.517 --> 00:04:19.076
When data doesn't come as records, implement IDataSource yourself.

00:04:19.076 --> 00:04:25.027
GetDataSetsAsync lists the data sets the user may read, and GetSchemaAsync describes one

00:04:25.027 --> 00:04:26.927
of them with its fields.

00:04:27.677 --> 00:04:30.439
GetSchemaAsync is the security boundary.

00:04:30.439 --> 00:04:34.462
It returns null for a data set that doesn't exist or that the

00:04:34.462 --> 00:04:39.627
user may not read, and consumers read a data set only after it returns a schema.

00:04:40.377 --> 00:04:42.392
ReadAsync yields batches.

00:04:42.392 --> 00:04:46.942
A batch holds the fields and the rows, with one value per field,

00:04:46.942 --> 00:04:51.102
in order, using the .NET type that matches the field type.

00:04:51.102 --> 00:04:56.237
It checks the access of the user again, and yields nothing when it fails.

00:05:00.437 --> 00:05:05.501
A data source that reads changes from a queue should remove them only once delivered.

00:05:05.501 --> 00:05:07.338
A step passes a ConsumerId.

00:05:07.338 --> 00:05:11.456
OnSucceeded adds an action that runs only if the step read every row

00:05:11.456 --> 00:05:15.297
and the run succeeds, so a failed run's changes are read again.

00:05:16.047 --> 00:05:20.604
A data source that can group and aggregate in its own store implements

00:05:20.604 --> 00:05:25.520
IDataAggregateSource, and answers null to any query it can't answer exactly.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:30.737
The documentation of Data Sources also covers file formats and formula functions.

00:05:32.737 --> 00:05:34.247
That is a data source:

00:05:34.247 --> 00:05:38.886
a data set that declares its fields and who may read them, and one registration.

00:05:38.886 --> 00:05:43.094
Every consumer of data sources can then read your module's data, for the

00:05:43.094 --> 00:05:44.604
users who may read it.

00:05:44.604 --> 00:05:46.007
Thanks for watching.

